+# Full study: Arxiv.«2001.02665».ringel_conjecture_large
+
+## Frozen object
+
+The target is the exact `ringel_conjecture_large` declaration from pinned Formal Conjectures revision `2270d31e8dd611521f979de6d86da364930b7669`, source span `FormalConjectures/Arxiv/2001.02665/RingelConjecture.lean:37-53`. The provider declaration carries `sorryAx`; its bytes are statement authority only.
+
+```lean
+∀ᶠ (n : ℕ) in Filter.atTop, ∀ {V : Type} [Finite V] (T : SimpleGraph V),
+      T.IsTree → T.edgeSet.ncard = n →
+      ∃ f : Fin (2 * n + 1) → (V ↪ Fin (2 * n + 1)),
+        Pairwise (fun i j => Disjoint (T.map (f i)).edgeSet (T.map (f j)).edgeSet) ∧
+        ⨆ i, T.map (f i) = (⊤ : SimpleGraph (Fin (2 * n + 1)))
+```
+
+## Resolution frontier
+
+The exact statement and both definitional transports are machine-checked. The substantive result is the deep Montgomery–Pokrovskiy–Sudakov large-n tree-decomposition theorem; no corresponding trust-zero theorem is present in the pinned local libraries.
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:BEGIN -->
+### PU-00
+The pinned source record fixes the eventual Ringel statement: for all sufficiently large natural n, every finite tree with n edges has 2n+1 edge-disjoint copies covering the complete graph. `Target` is definitionally equal to `FrozenStatement`.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:BEGIN -->
+### PU-01
+The outer `Filter.Eventually` obligation requires one threshold and a uniform construction beyond it; finitely many checked instances cannot discharge it.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:BEGIN -->
+### PU-02
+At each tail index n the local premises are a finite vertex type, `T.IsTree`, and `T.edgeSet.ncard = n`. These premises state the intended decomposition but do not construct witnesses.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:BEGIN -->
+### PU-03
+The pairwise-disjointness conjunct is a global combinatorial packing assertion not implied by `IsTree` or the edge count alone.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:BEGIN -->
+### PU-04
+The supremum equality requires the copies to cover every edge of the complete graph. Counting is necessary but does not provide an embedding family or prove equality.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:BEGIN -->
+### PU-05
+A completed root would compose the embedding family, disjointness, and cover obligations into the existential conclusion for each sufficiently large n.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:BEGIN -->
+### PU-06
+The provider theorem cannot be used because its body is `sorryAx`-bearing; the deep large-n construction remains an explicit cut.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-ROOT-OPEN:BEGIN -->
+### PU-ROOT-OPEN
+The exact machine root remains open. This handoff records the missing proof rather than importing a bodyless oracle or claiming theorem completion.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-ROOT-OPEN:END -->
+
+## Conclusion
+
+This is a provisional self-tested nonacceptance handoff. `machine_complete` and `theorem_complete` remain false; the canonical Master must preserve the cut and may accept only a future independently checked exact root.

